Let the device chooser survive the press that was meant for it.

navigator.bluetooth.requestDevice() listed devices and then dismissed itself on
any touch: not only on a device row, but on the chooser's own title, where
there is nothing to click. The page got "NotFoundError: User cancelled the
requestDevice() chooser" every time, and the first call after the OS
permission dialog failed the same way without the chooser ever appearing.

Instrumentation (patches/ohos-bubble-input-diagnostics.patch) put the sequence
beyond doubt. On a press inside the chooser:

  bubble activation name=ChooserBubbleUiViewDelegate active=0 close_on_deactivate=1
  widget close      name=ChooserBubbleUiViewDelegate reason=3        <- kLostFocus
  aura target       target=RenderWidgetHostViewAura                  <- 1ms later

and on a press inside the geolocation prompt, which works:

  bubble activation name=PermissionPromptBubbleBaseView active=0 close_on_deactivate=0
  aura target       target=PermissionPromptBubbleBaseView
  bubble activation name=PermissionPromptBubbleBaseView active=1

The press deactivates both. HarmonyOS draws the browser in one XComponent
surface and bubbles get no logical window of their own, so a press resolves to
the browser's window and activates it, and Views reports that as the bubble
losing focus -- even when the press is inside the bubble. The prompt rides it
out because permission_prompt_bubble_base_view.cc already calls
set_close_on_deactivate(false); the chooser was destroyed in the millisecond
before WindowTargeter ran, which is why the event then landed on the page.

The two bubbles are otherwise identical at creation, field for field:
has_parent=1, has_anchor_view=0, accept_events=1, activatable=1, the same
anchor rect and the same z-order sublevel. So this is the difference, and the
fix is to make the chooser say what the prompt already says.

Not the underlying quirk. A press inside a bubble should not deactivate it,
and until that is fixed any bubble that keeps the default will behave this
way. Fixing it properly means changing how this port assigns activation, which
is a larger change against a much less certain payoff.

Generated against Chromium 154.0.8037.51.

Apply with:
  git -C /path/to/chromium/src apply /path/to/patches/ohos-web-bluetooth-chooser-input.patch

diff --git a/chrome/browser/ui/views/permissions/chooser_bubble_ui.cc b/chrome/browser/ui/views/permissions/chooser_bubble_ui.cc
index bbfbf51..d688e5e 100644
--- a/chrome/browser/ui/views/permissions/chooser_bubble_ui.cc
+++ b/chrome/browser/ui/views/permissions/chooser_bubble_ui.cc
@@ -7,6 +7,7 @@
 #include "base/functional/callback_helpers.h"
 #include "base/memory/raw_ptr.h"
 #include "base/memory/weak_ptr.h"
+#include "build/build_config.h"
 #include "chrome/browser/picture_in_picture/picture_in_picture_occlusion_tracker.h"
 #include "chrome/browser/picture_in_picture/picture_in_picture_window_manager.h"
 #include "chrome/browser/platform_util.h"
@@ -155,6 +156,26 @@ ChooserBubbleUiViewDelegate::ChooserBubbleUiViewDelegate(
   SetCloseCallback(
       base::BindOnce(&DeviceChooserContentView::Close,
                      base::Unretained(device_chooser_content_view_)));
+
+#if BUILDFLAG(IS_OHOS)
+  // A press inside this bubble deactivates it before anything is targeted.
+  //
+  // HarmonyOS draws the browser in one XComponent surface and bubbles get no
+  // logical window of their own, so every press resolves to the browser's and
+  // activates it. Device traces show both this bubble and the geolocation
+  // prompt being deactivated by the press that was meant for them, about a
+  // millisecond before WindowTargeter runs. The prompt survives because
+  // PermissionPromptBubbleBaseView already sets this flag; this bubble closed
+  // instead, with ClosedReason::kLostFocus, and the targeter then found the
+  // page underneath -- so a tap anywhere inside the chooser, including its own
+  // title, dismissed it and requestDevice() rejected with
+  // "NotFoundError: User cancelled the requestDevice() chooser".
+  //
+  // Losing the close-on-deactivate behaviour costs little here: this port has
+  // no other window to click away to, and the chooser still closes through
+  // Cancel, Escape and the close button.
+  set_close_on_deactivate(false);
+#endif
 }
 
 ChooserBubbleUiViewDelegate::~ChooserBubbleUiViewDelegate() = default;
